                "entry_id": "e149defc-6475-3b70-80cb-b125c08cb4b5",
                "word": "endoste",
                "pos": "noun",
                "lang_code": "fr",
                "forms": [
                    {
                        "form_id": "bbc366a1-b652-4fb3-aa1c-76d89af1d048",
                        "entry_id": "e149defc-6475-3b70-80cb-b125c08cb4b5",
                        "tags": [
                            "plural"
                        ],
                        "form": "endostes"
                    }
                ],
                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "96c52d07-f52c-38f4-a7bb-d44ea4fa7b7b",
                        "entry_id": "e149defc-6475-3b70-80cb-b125c08cb4b5",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Aussi appelé membrane médullaire, l’endoste est une fine couche de tissu conjonctif qui tapisse l’ensemble des cavités vascularisées d’un os : les cavités médullaires, les canaux de Havers, les canaux de Volkmann et les cavités de l’os spongieux. Sa structure est la même que celle de la couche profonde du périoste : ostéoblastique non fibreuse riche en cellules."
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "105cba4e-093c-43a0-a776-f21b50a4cf1a",
                                "sense_id": "96c52d07-f52c-38f4-a7bb-d44ea4fa7b7b",
                                "text": "La surface interne des os est bordée d’une membrane mal définie et très largement cellulaire, que l’on appelle l’endoste. Tant le périoste que l’endoste sont des tissus ostéogéniques. Ils contiennent des cellules osseuses ayant une activité génératrice d’os. Elles sont nombreuses et actives chez les sujets juvéniles. Ces cellules se réduisent en nombre chez l’adulte, tout en restant potentiellement actives. Elles peuvent être stimulées et déposer de la substance osseuse quand le périoste est traumatisé."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
                "translations": [
                    {
                        "translation_id": "5b86cd56-fd8e-4fc4-a91c-6a18c91bebac",
                        "source_entry_id": "e149defc-6475-3b70-80cb-b125c08cb4b5",
                        "source_sense_id": "96c52d07-f52c-38f4-a7bb-d44ea4fa7b7b",
                        "target_lang_word": "Endost",
                        "target_lang_code": "de",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": []
                    },
                    {
                        "translation_id": "00390f3c-7f2e-4a86-9fdf-682b26ff1cb7",
                        "source_entry_id": "e149defc-6475-3b70-80cb-b125c08cb4b5",
                        "source_sense_id": "96c52d07-f52c-38f4-a7bb-d44ea4fa7b7b",
                        "target_lang_word": "endosteum",
                        "target_lang_code": "en",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": [
                            {
                                "target_word": "endosteum",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "d2328627-33f7-3d13-80d4-7cee4559f24b",
                                        "entry_id": "7a8ec795-4b8f-3ce5-aa1c-4daa09aee9a6",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "A membranous vascular layer of cells which line the medullary cavity of a bone; an internal periosteum."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "6465c8e6-8b37-4cc6-9330-bd01e4fc647d",
                                                "sense_id": "d2328627-33f7-3d13-80d4-7cee4559f24b",
                                                "text": "Hyaline cartilage is insensitive, and radiographic changes imputed to it can have no bearing on pain from it unless it is so worn away that the endosteums of the articulating bones are rubbing on each other."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "c7b26ff6-6835-46e8-8b44-1e7e26757182",
                                                "sense_id": "d2328627-33f7-3d13-80d4-7cee4559f24b",
                                                "text": "The cortical and trabecular endosteums are the inner linings of bone. The osteonal endosteum lines the osteonal canals […] and is the internal lining of bone. It is continuous with the periosteal and cortical endosteums."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "022f04f9-87da-4d73-9816-30c89d29bb48",
                                                "sense_id": "d2328627-33f7-3d13-80d4-7cee4559f24b",
                                                "text": "Periosteum and endosteum are connective tissue sheaths of bone. […] The endosteum is a less well-defined layer of connective tissue and osteogenic cells applied to the wall of a bony cavity. / a. The endosteum lies between the bone tissue and the bone marrow. / b. Unlike periosteum, endosteum lacks a distinct fibrous layer."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "b3c39672-6ba1-43d1-9530-a45e26c66c9f",
                                                "sense_id": "d2328627-33f7-3d13-80d4-7cee4559f24b",
                                                "text": "The periosteum is constrained and interrupted by tendons, ligaments, and fibrocartilage, whereas the endosteum is awash with hematopoitic bone marrow[…]."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "54fe5f66-4b84-4594-bdb4-9e287fd41b8b",
                                                "sense_id": "d2328627-33f7-3d13-80d4-7cee4559f24b",
                                                "text": "Tibial endostea isolated from 19-day-old chick embroys and cultured for 10 days became populated with multinucleated cells that arise from monocytes."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            }
                        ]
                    },
